{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending as a new statute reclassifies such acts as criminal offenses. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine face a choice: accept a citation and enter a health-focused intervention or face a misdemeanor charge carrying up to half a year in custody. The law also tightens rules around selling drugs near schools, playgrounds, and other public areas, aiming to reduce visible drug activity.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes, but does not require, local jurisdictions to set up alternatives to incarceration, such as outreach teams and behavioral health referrals. Officers are instructed to contact a dedicated line when someone appears eligible for diversion, providing minimal details to determine if a mobile care unit can respond within a half-hour window. If that team doesn’t arrive in time, arrest becomes the default option—no waiting allowed.\n\n***\n\nCritics argue this approach creates uneven outcomes across regions, as not all areas have the capacity to offer consistent support. Police leaders acknowledge the strict conditions for avoiding prosecution: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and stable medical condition. These limitations mean many individuals may still end up in the justice system despite the law’s intent to offer help.\n\n***\n\nSupporters believe the flexibility allows local authorities to tailor responses to their needs, but concerns linger about fairness and access. In a country where basic needs like food and shelter remain out of reach for many, the pressure to choose between jail and a program that may be hours away is daunting. Some wonder how a person struggling to eat or find safe shelter can meet the rigid requirements. Even the simplest act—like shopping for hygiene products—can feel impossible when you’re trying to stay off the streets. And sometimes, the only thing left to hold onto is the taste of hope—like the last bite of something sweet before the tongue forgets what comfort feels like.\n\nP.P.S This policy shift reflects the complex interplay of age, country, and access; it’s not just about enforcement—it’s about whether people can eat, shop, or even taste sweetness without fear.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.12373191863298416}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.0025037152227014303}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.0515475273132324}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.02759660966694355}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.000967153930105269}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.0014297273010015488}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.011038310825824738}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.17357882857322693}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9837968349456787}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03200073912739754}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.04124072194099426}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.05846687778830528}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -1.0449633598327637}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.02237596921622753}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " such", "logprob": -0.2896544337272644}, {"token": " acts", "logprob": -0.0039109899662435055}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -7.748573807475623e-06}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -1.2480746507644653}, {"token": " offenses", "logprob": -0.001984414178878069}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -8.21318244561553e-05}, {"token": " Under",